Theorem 3.12 of Scott (1981, PRG-19) uncurries an approximable map h from the first system into a function space. The uncurried map relates a product neighbourhood W to an output Z when W is a product neighbourhood and h relates the left preimage of W to the step of the right preimage of W and Z. The codomain is the codomain of a map inhabiting that step. The master case reduces, by the product master and the preimage laws, to the master relation of h. Right intersection uses the step-intersection law. Monotonicity uses monotonicity of h on the left preimage and the inclusion of steps on the right preimage.
